\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Call a finite point set $S$ Euclidean Ramsey if, for every finite number of colors, some Euclidean dimension has the property that every coloring of the whole space contains a monochromatic isometric copy of $S$. Must every finite set that lies on a sphere be Euclidean Ramsey? The four-point subsets of a circle are included.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://people.math.sc.edu/cooper/combprob.html}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Call a finite point set $S$ Euclidean Ramsey if, for every finite number of colors, some Euclidean dimension has the property that every coloring of the whole space contains a monochromatic isometric copy of $S$. Must every finite set that lies on a sphere be Euclidean Ramsey? The four-point subsets of a circle are included.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 3100002 (AMR-030-0002). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://people.math.sc.edu/cooper/combprob.html}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:3100002}

